\section{Structural Manifold Invariants in Relativistic Engines}

Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the structural manifold of the engine. TSSE requires:
\[
\forall t,\quad \mathcal{U}(t)\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Relativistic Structural Manifold]
A relativistic structural manifold $\mathcal{S}$ is a closed, invariant subset of
phase-space satisfying:
\[
\mathcal{S} = \{(q,p) \mid F(q,p) = 0\},
\]
for some invariant constraint function $F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Relativistic Manifold Closure]
If $\mathcal{U}(t)$ is symplectic and TSSE-admissible, then:
\[
F(q(t),p(t)) = 0 \quad \forall t.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Symplectic evolution preserves constraint surfaces defined by first integrals.
Since $F$ is invariant under $\mathcal{U}(t)$, closure follows.
\end{proof}

This lemma ensures that relativistic TSSE engines maintain structural stability under all admissible evolutions.

